  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeformation stretches from Hudson Bay low pressure into the southern \nPlains, observed as a continuous band of cloud cover and line of \nconvection that bisects mid Michigan. This boundary will very slowly \ndrift into SE Michigan this evening and overnight, and has already \nstarted to generate low-topped showers over SW Lower MI and a line of\nconvection across northern lower MI. So far have seen limited\nevidence of deep convective growth, effectively limiting severe \nchances this evening. The dry airmass is even more prevalent \nsoutheast of the cloud shield, which currently spans from Sturgis- \nHowell-Bad Axe and is a good delineation for rain potential this \nevening. While the cloud shield is expected to expand east into the \nDetroit Metro region overnight, rain chances hold off until \novernight-early Thursday morning given the dry column in place (PW \n~1.6\" at MBS versus ~0.6\" at DTW). Even then, duration of rain \nchances will mainly be tied to a glancing bout of moisture advection \nas a wave travels from Iowa to northern Michigan early Thursday \nmorning. Thus highest PoPs exist in the Tri Cities and Thumb. \n\nHigher confidence cloud cover keeps overnight lows several degrees \nabove normal--in the mid to upper 60s, which is close to afternoon\ndewpoints across the Tri Cities and Thumb regions. MOS guidance is\nhighlighting a brief window for fog around daybreak, mainly across\nthe north where rainfall has saturated soils. \n\nThe surface trough lingers just to our west Thursday, keeping a weak \nconvergence zone in tact before fully washing out Friday. This \nbrings slightly cooler air into the region both due to the leftover \npost-frontal airmass and dynamic cooling, with highs forecast to be \nin the low 80s Thursday and mid 80s Friday. Thermodynamic profiles \nare right on the cusp of being unstable both days, but may struggle \nto break the cap as mid-level dry air surges in behind the departing \nwave. The best opportunities for convection both days will thus come \nif one of the many perturbations in the wave train slides through \nduring the peak heating cycle. This provides the best support for \nmid-level moisture/cooling as well as a more organized wind field \nfor updraft sustenance. Not out of the question for a stronger cell \nto produce gusty winds, but this is an outlier scenario in the model \ndistribution. Periods of dry weather are expected to emerge between \nepisodes of rain, and may even see some pockets of sunshine. \n\nLongwave troughing stays entrenched across eastern CONUS through the \nweekend, transitioning to a zonal pattern aloft by early next week \nas the Atlantic high dislodges. Continued influence of the high \nthrough the weekend maintains a gulf moisture connection, keeping \nconditions unsettled as the wave train also persists. This supports \na continued pattern of oscillating wet and dry periods into the \nweekend, while convective influence makes it difficult to pinpoint \ntiming. Models at the moment are targeting late morning-early \nevening timing on Saturday for a frontal passage, which would be the \nmost focused period of ascent this weekend. High temperatures stay \nseasonable in the mid 80s through the weekend as well.",
